12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the principles and architecture of Hardware Description Language \(HDL\) Linting and Static Rule Checking, detailing how linters catch synthesis-simulation mismatches, unclocked latches, and clock-domain crossings.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Motivation for Static RTL Linting:) Tj
T*
(   - Compiling and simulating Verilog/SystemVerilog RTL code does not guarantee that the design) Tj
T*
(   will synthesize cleanly or operate reliably on silicon. Verilog is weakly typed and notoriously) Tj
T*
(   permissive, allowing coding errors \(such as implicit wire declarations or incomplete) Tj
T*
(   sensitivity lists\) that simulate without errors but produce non-functional physical silicon.) Tj
T*
(   - Automated RTL linters \(e.g., Synopsys SpyGlass, Real Intent Ascent\) perform deep static) Tj
T*
(   analysis of RTL code before simulation, catching critical architectural flaws.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Critical Flaws Detected by RTL Linters:) Tj
T*
(   - Unintentional Latches: In combinational always blocks, if an output signal is not explicitly) Tj
T*
(   assigned in all branches of an if-else or case statement, hardware synthesis infers a) Tj
T*
(   transparent level-sensitive latch instead of pure combinational gates. Latches are timing) Tj
T*
(   hazards and cause severe testability defects.) Tj
T*
(   - Synthesis-Simulation Mismatches: Incomplete always @\(*\) sensitivity lists or race conditions) Tj
T*
(   between blocking and non-blocking assignments cause simulation behavior to differ completely) Tj
T*
ET

